/* ============================================================================
complex-conjugate.epher -- conj(zc), and zc conj(zc) = |zc|^2

The conjugate of a + bi is a - bi, the reflection across the real axis;
multiplying zc by its conjugate clears the imaginary part:
(a + bi)(a - bi) = a^2 + b^2 = |zc|^2 (Churchill and Brown, "Complex
Variables and Applications", sec. 5). The product comes out as the exact
real numbers 25 and 2 below, and the identity is checked against |zc|^2.
All values are exact.

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/algebra/complex/complex-conjugate.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\algebra\complex\complex-conjugate.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/algebra/complex/complex-conjugate.epher

No knobs to edit; the values below are fixed examples.
============================================================================ */

// ---- the examples -----------------------------------------------------------
const zc = 3 + 4i
const w = 1 - i

// ---- results -----------------------------------------------------------------
print("conjugate:", conj(zc))
print("zc conj(zc):", zc * conj(zc))
print("|zc|^2:", abs(zc) ^ 2)
print("zc conj(zc) = |zc|^2:", abs(zc * conj(zc) - abs(zc) ^ 2) < 1e-12)
print("conjugate of 1 - i:", conj(w))
print("(1 - i)(1 + i):", w * conj(w))
/* ---- expected output ----
= 3+4i
= 1-i
= conjugate: 3-4i
= zc conj(zc): 25
= |zc|^2: 25
= zc conj(zc) = |zc|^2: true
= conjugate of 1 - i: 1+i
= (1 - i)(1 + i): 2
*/
